{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>And Esav said, I have plenty.</b> This implies 'plenty' — but not 'everything.' But Yaakov said, ״כִּי חַנַּנִי אֱלֹהִים וְכִי יֶשׁ לִי כֹל״ — 'for Elohim has been gracious to me and I have all.' This is because the wicked, even though they possess all the silver and gold in the world, nevertheless it always seems to them that they are still lacking — they have 'plenty' but not 'everything' they need. Therefore Esav said ״יֶשׁ לִי רָב״ — 'I have plenty,' but not 'all,' for they are still lacking; he who has a hundred mina craves two hundred. And the righteous are the reverse of this: even if they have but a little in their hands, they are satisfied with it and rejoice in their portion, and it seems to them as though they have everything. As it is written (Ecclesiastes 5:18): ״גַּם כָּל הָאָדָם אֲשֶׁר נָתַן לוֹ הָאֱלֹהִים עֹשֶׁר וּנְכָסִים וְהִשְׁלִיטוֹ לֶאֱכֹל מִמֶּנּוּ וְגוֹ׳ זוֹ מַתַּת אֱלֹהִים הִוא״. And afterward he said: ״אִישׁ אֲשֶׁר יִתֵּן לוֹ הָאֱלֹהִים עֹשֶׁר וּנְכָסִים וְכָבוֹד וְאֵינֶנּוּ חָסֵר לְנַפְשׁוֹ מִכֹּל אֲשֶׁר יִתְאַוֶּה וְלֹא יַשְׁלִיטֶנּוּ הָאֱלֹהִים לֶאֱכֹל מִמֶּנּוּ״ and so forth. And it is difficult: why did he first mention ״אֲשֶׁר נָתַן לוֹ הָאֱלֹהִים״ — in the past tense, and then afterward say ״אֲשֶׁר יִתֵּן לוֹ הָאֱלֹהִים״ — 'will give him,' which implies the future?" "But the explanation is that the first verse speaks of a righteous man, who does not distinguish between much and little — for even in the little he has, it seems to him as though Elohim has already given him everything. And such a man is master over his wealth, to do with it as he will, and Elohim has given him mastery over it, and so forth. But the second verse speaks of a wicked man who is evil, for whom the whole world is never enough to satisfy his need. For he is always seeking that HaShem will yet give him, in the future, more wealth and possessions, and he also seeks the imagined honor that is attained through abundance of wealth — unlike the good man before Elohim, who escapes and flees from honor. Therefore honor was not mentioned in the first verse, and only afterward was it mentioned regarding those who pursue abundance of wealth — even though in truth he lacks nothing of all that he desires and has in hand enough for his need, yet on all accounts he craves that HaShem give him still more. Such a man — Elohim will certainly not give him mastery to eat of it, for his wealth is master over him: wherever it wills, it leads him, but he is not master over his wealth, and his eye is grudging with his own. And he has an officer appointed over him who does not let him touch his wealth to take from it and give it to charity or for his own needs. Therefore it is said of Esav ״יֶשׁ לִי רָב״ (Genesis 33:9), and of Yaakov ״יֶשׁ לִי כֹל״ (Genesis 33:11). And he used the expression ״חַנַּנִי״ — 'has been gracious to me' — for the righteous never rely on their own good deeds, and they think that everything given them is solely Elohim's kindness all day long, given to them by way of grace. But the wicked, for the most part, think themselves to be righteous and believe that everything was given to them by way of obligation." "And in Midrash Tanchuma (Leviticus 10:7) Rabbi Yitzchak said: The wicked have no true good in this world, as it is said, ״וְטוֹב לֹא יִהְיֶה לָרָשָׁע״ (Ecclesiastes 8:13). They raise an objection: but is it not written ״כְּטוֹב לֵב הַמֶּלֶךְ בַּיָּיִן״! He said to them: ״בְּטוֹב״ — 'good' — is not written, but rather ״כְּטוֹב״ — 'like good' — good that is not good. But regarding Israel it is written (I Kings 8:66) ״וַיֵּלְכוּ לְאָהֳלֵיהֶם שְׂמֵחִים וְטוֹבֵי לֵב עַל כָּל הַטּוֹבָה״ — actual good. And this agrees with our words, that the wicked, in all the great bounties they receive, it seems to them like good that is not good — as though they are still lacking. But regarding the righteous it is written ״עַל כָּל הַטּוֹבָה״ — 'upon all the good' — for it always seems to them that they have everything. Therefore Yaakov said ״כִּי חַנַּנִי אֱלֹהִים וְכִי יֶשׁ לִי כֹל״. And some say: here Esav acknowledged to him concerning the blessings, and said ״יֶשׁ לִי רָב״ — I acknowledge that you are my master (rav) and I am the servant. And if so, what would you give me? For whatever a servant acquires, his master acquires."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:32:06.095169688Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}